00:01:57.015 --> 00:02:01.788
You should always carry it with
you with the periodic table.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:06.759
Now, on Monday,
this is what we had.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.862
This was a conduction band.

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:10.663
This was a valance band.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:16.236
And then we had a
conduction band and valence

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:20.640
band that were a little
bit closer together.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.412
And then, we had this
case, where maybe it's

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:29.349
filled up somewhere in
the middle of the band.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.918
And remember, the valance
band is filled with electrons,

00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:40.560
and the conduction band is not,
right, in the simplest picture.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.965
And this difference in
energy between this VBN--

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.602
that's the VBN, the maximum
level of the valence band--

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.304
and the CBN--

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.273
the minimum level of
the conduction band--

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:59.245
that energy difference is
the gap of the material.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:04.117
Here, you can see
energy gap equals zero.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.286
Well, in this
case, it's a metal.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.353
Or if you want,
it's a conductor.

00:03:08.353 --> 00:03:12.759
And we'll talk about why it's
a conductor more on Friday,

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.827
but a little bit today,
why metals are conductors.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.797
Today, I really want
to focus on this case.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.533
All right, so that's
a metal when there

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:24.270
is no gap in the material.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.007
If the gap is large, then
that is an insulator.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:33.579
What do I mean by large?

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:39.085
Well, what I mean is it's not in
the range of this special case,

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:42.956
which would be, say,
zero less than the gap

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:47.360
less than around
3.5 electron volts.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.064
This is the case that I
want to talk about today.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:53.900
I want to spend today
talking about semiconductors.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.070
Those are called semiconductors.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.038
Let's write that here.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.106
Semiconductors.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.050
Sometimes you'll see
them called metaloids.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.521
Sometimes you'll see them called
insulators with small gaps.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:16.589
They're all kind of right.

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.996
Metaloid, thank you.

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:28.067
Now, the reason why we classify
these and why this range of gap

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.638
is so important is what I
want to convey to you today.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:33.573
These are really
special materials,

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:36.175
these semiconductors.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:42.382
Now, before I do that, I want
to just clarify one thing.

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:47.453
And I'm going to use diamond
and graphite as an example.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:51.324
On Monday, what we
did is we talked

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:57.163
about the concept of bands with
a very simple 1D model, right?

00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:04.570
This was a 1D model of
s orbitals, 1s orbitals.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:09.175
I said, well, if I have
a mole of 1s orbitals,

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:13.780
then that goes into a band.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.317
And I called it a 1s band.

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:18.918
I always say, well,
if you fill it

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.888
in different ways, that is
what determines the properties.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:24.190
But I want to be clear-- and
I stressed this on Monday,

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:27.694
and I want to stress it again.

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:31.230
Whether this forms this
and then the next set--

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.232
you know, whether
you get a 1s that

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.335
forms a band like
this and then a 2s

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:38.438
that forms a band like
this and then another one,

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:40.707
maybe 2p, like this.

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.609
Or whether there's
breaks in them,

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:45.778
or maybe these kind of overlap--

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:50.316
so you get overlap, and you get
sort of something like that.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:57.223
All that depends on more than
just those atomic orbitals.

00:05:57.223 --> 00:05:58.191
It depends on more.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.259
It depends on how these
things are bonded.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.628
This is such a great example.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:04.664
I've got the same element here.

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.133
Its carbon.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:08.935
There's nothing
else in this system.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.036
Carbon, pure carbon.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:11.437
Everywhere is carbon.

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:14.674
But on the one hand, I've
arranged it in a certain way.

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:17.343
And on the other hand, I've
arranged it in another way,

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:21.814
and their properties
couldn't be more different.

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:28.154
So just to take this
a little further,

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:33.893
if I think about diamond on the
left, diamond is an insulator.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:37.463
Diamond has a gap of 5
and 1/2 electronvolts.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:38.931
5 and 1/2 electronvolts.

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:41.300
It's an insulator.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.569
But OK, that makes
me think of methane.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:46.139
Remember, methane, you
had these SP3 states--

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:51.944
SP3, SP3, SP3.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:58.151
And the reason it formed SP3
is because all these H1s states

00:06:58.151 --> 00:06:59.652
came along--

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:07.293
H, H, H, H. These are 1s's, 1s.

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:10.897
And so carbon came along, and
it said, well, if I arrange--

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:17.336
if I got four of you here like
this and I arrange myself--

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.539
if I arrange my
orbitals in such a way

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:23.576
that then I can arrange so that
they're minimizing repulsions,

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:24.710
that gives me methane.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.446
That gives me the
ground state of methane.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.981
But the only way to do that
is to have four equivalent

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:29.682
orbitals.

00:07:29.682 --> 00:07:31.951
Those are hybrid orbitals.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.887
We talked about this when we
talked about hybridization.

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:39.058
The idea being that if I took
one of these and one of those--

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:41.360
so now you can imagine
how it's going to look.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.296
You know all this now, right?

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:46.566
There's a 1s
orbital of hydrogen.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:52.071
You know that those will
form a sigma and a sigma star

00:07:52.071 --> 00:07:54.407
and occupy that bonding orbital.

00:07:54.407 --> 00:07:56.509
That's the idea of methane.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.945
That's the idea of
molecular orbital theory

00:07:58.945 --> 00:08:01.747
with some hybridization.

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:04.817
And it's going to form those
bonds along each direction.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:07.386
But now I'm talking about
something different.

00:08:07.386 --> 00:08:10.389
I'm not talking about
such a simple case.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:16.762
I'm talking about
one mole of SP3.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.498
That's what diamond is.

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.601
Look, the carbon atoms
have done the same thing.

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:24.103
They said, hey, wait a second,
OK, it's not four hydrogens.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:29.075
It's like 10 to the 23 carbons
all in one small space.

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:33.712
How can we organize
to lower our energy?

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:34.780
And so they form--

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.317
well, we know this, that
it's going to form a band.

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:43.389
But it doesn't just form a
band and then half fill it.

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:45.758
That's not what diamond does.

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:50.296
No, actually, diamond
breaks this apart so

00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:54.500
that you have a huge gap.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.338
And those are filled,
and then those are not.

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:05.478
The SP3 band in
diamond breaks apart

00:09:05.478 --> 00:09:08.981
into a valence and
a conduction band.

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:10.416
The valance band is full.

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:12.418
The conduction band is empty.

00:09:12.418 --> 00:09:12.919
Why?

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:15.888
Well, you don't need to
learn the specifics of how

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:17.023
to get from there to there.

00:09:17.023 --> 00:09:19.992
But I wanted to show you
how complicated this is.

00:09:19.992 --> 00:09:24.964
I will ask you whether the
SP3 band splits or not.

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.133
I just wanted to show you
how dependent this is.

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:30.703
Because now, look, you can
understand this now, right?

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:32.672
So I've got four electrons
per atom going here.

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:35.474
There's a huge gap, and
then nothing in there.

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:36.576
That's diamond.

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:40.680
5.5 eV gap.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.116
One mole of SP3 in diamond.

00:09:44.116 --> 00:09:46.218
Now, it depends
on the structure.

00:09:46.218 --> 00:09:49.188
If I had just put them
together in some amorphous soup

00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:50.289
with no--

00:09:50.289 --> 00:09:51.524
oh, amorphous.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:53.793
That's a word
that's coming later.

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:56.562
But if I just put them
together with no order,

00:09:56.562 --> 00:09:59.865
I wouldn't get this,
this splitting.

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:03.102
You take graphene, which
is one of those sheets,

00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:05.271
and make stacks and
you've got graphite.

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:07.840
It's another form
of pure carbon.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:12.278
In that case, you've got your
SP2 bonds to form in the plane,

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:14.780
and those also
split and they fill.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:21.287
But now you've got your pi
bonds, and those don't split.

00:10:21.287 --> 00:10:28.694
So in graphite, what you get
is you've got your SP2's.

00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:33.032
You've got a mole of those,
and you've got your P electron.

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:34.533
Remember those?

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:38.571
Now you do a mole of that,
and you've got the same thing.

00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:40.873
But see now, you've got--

00:10:40.873 --> 00:10:42.274
these are the SP2 bands.

00:10:44.944 --> 00:10:47.179
It also split.

00:10:47.179 --> 00:10:49.348
Remember, I've got
my electrons here,

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:51.450
my SP2 electrons
and those there.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:57.023
But now, I've got the pi
bands that didn't split.

00:10:57.023 --> 00:10:59.859
Graphite is a metal.

00:10:59.859 --> 00:11:03.462
You can understand it
by looking at the bands

00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:05.364
of these materials.

00:11:05.364 --> 00:11:07.299
Now, again, let me
emphasize, I am not

00:11:07.299 --> 00:11:09.835
going to ask you to
know when something

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.205
splits like this or not.

00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:17.543
Why didn't the pi band split
into two like these did?

00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:21.213
When does an S and a P become
a single band versus an S

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:22.715
band separated by a P band?

00:11:22.715 --> 00:11:24.283
I'm not going to ask
you to know that,

00:11:24.283 --> 00:11:25.951
but I wanted you to see it.

00:11:25.951 --> 00:11:27.953
It doesn't just depend
on the initial states.

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:31.557
It depends on the
structure of those atoms.

00:11:31.557 --> 00:11:35.728
It depends on how those
atoms are bonded together.

00:11:35.728 --> 00:11:42.134
Now, what about
this semi-conductor?

00:11:42.134 --> 00:11:44.003
What about this semiconductor?

00:11:44.003 --> 00:11:48.641
Well, if we take a semiconductor
and we look at it--

00:11:48.641 --> 00:11:49.775
I told you this on Monday.

00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:54.714
There are two things that
you can do in your goody bag,

00:11:54.714 --> 00:11:55.614
especially.

00:11:55.614 --> 00:12:01.754
One is you can take an electron,
and you can shine a light

00:12:01.754 --> 00:12:06.759
on it, and it's going to
excite this electron up, just

00:12:06.759 --> 00:12:08.561
like in the Bohr atom, right?

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:12.732
You can absorb photons if the
photon energy is the energy

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:14.800
between one state and another.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.335
We did a lot of this earlier.

00:12:16.335 --> 00:12:20.740
But see, in a
semiconductor, remember,

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.075
I've got an almost infinite
number of states here.

00:12:25.711 --> 00:12:28.013
And so what I need
is for that photon--

00:12:28.013 --> 00:12:29.849
there's not an exact value here.

00:12:29.849 --> 00:12:31.550
There's a continuum.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.153
But here, it's forbidden,
just like in the Bohr atom.

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:35.187
There's no states.

00:12:35.187 --> 00:12:37.022
There's no electron.

00:12:37.022 --> 00:12:45.264
So here, the energy of
the photon absorbed--

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:48.100
let's write this out--

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:52.171
has to be greater than
the energy of the gap.

00:12:52.171 --> 00:12:56.108
This is the energy of the gap.

00:12:56.108 --> 00:12:58.410
This is the energy of the gap.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:02.214
Now, by the same token, I can
take the same semiconductor

00:13:02.214 --> 00:13:04.383
and run it the other way.

00:13:04.383 --> 00:13:08.988
I can put a current on
it and feed electrons,

00:13:08.988 --> 00:13:12.057
electrons from current.

00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:20.599
So I can attach it to a
battery, and there you go.

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:23.869
This is a semiconductor
where I'm literally--

00:13:23.869 --> 00:13:27.840
all I'm doing is I'm feeding
electrons into its conduction

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.275
band.

00:13:29.275 --> 00:13:32.645
But now, when those
electrons fall down

00:13:32.645 --> 00:13:38.184
into the valence band,
they emit a photon.

00:13:38.184 --> 00:13:39.885
Photon emitted.

00:13:42.755 --> 00:13:49.929
And just so I have room, with
energy of the photon emitted

00:13:49.929 --> 00:13:53.465
is going to be exactly equal
to the energy of the gap.

00:13:53.465 --> 00:13:56.101
You can see that
because these electrons,

00:13:56.101 --> 00:13:58.370
I might inject them into the
conduction band anywhere,

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:01.040
but they're going to
wind up very, very,

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:06.278
very quickly going to this
minimum conduction band state.

00:14:06.278 --> 00:14:08.380
And that gets me to another
important point, which

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:12.251
is on the absorption side,
I can overshoot this.

00:14:12.251 --> 00:14:14.887
Like I said, I can absorb
higher than the gap.

00:14:14.887 --> 00:14:19.225
I can't absorb lower,
but I can absorb higher.

00:14:19.225 --> 00:14:24.196
And if I do, if I absorb
higher than the gap--

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:28.000
so let's say this is my state.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.904
So if my photon comes in and
it kicks in electron up really

00:14:31.904 --> 00:14:36.508
high, it's a high-energy photon,
then that electron very quickly

00:14:36.508 --> 00:14:38.277
will go back down.

00:14:38.277 --> 00:14:42.114
And that's called
thermalization.

00:14:42.114 --> 00:14:49.688
So what happens is that
electron loses energy

00:14:49.688 --> 00:14:55.561
to heat to get to the CBM.

00:14:58.864 --> 00:15:01.133
We'll come back to this later.

00:15:01.133 --> 00:15:02.101
That's thermalization.

00:15:02.101 --> 00:15:06.472
So the electron can be kicked
up by light to any level,

00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:08.807
but it's going to very,
very quickly go to the CBM.

00:15:08.807 --> 00:15:10.409
That's why when I
inject it-- that's

00:15:10.409 --> 00:15:12.444
why these are the same color.

00:15:12.444 --> 00:15:17.082
These are high-quality
LEDs, clearly.

00:15:17.082 --> 00:15:17.983
Well, they work.

00:15:17.983 --> 00:15:20.052
That's a good thing.

00:15:20.052 --> 00:15:22.121
So the batteries might
be slightly different,

00:15:22.121 --> 00:15:24.223
and the currents might be
different, a little bit.

00:15:24.223 --> 00:15:26.458
I might be injecting
electrons at different parts

00:15:26.458 --> 00:15:28.694
of these conduction bands,
but this is always green,

00:15:28.694 --> 00:15:29.795
and this is always red.

00:15:29.795 --> 00:15:33.565
And that's because those
electrons very quickly get down

00:15:33.565 --> 00:15:34.767
to the CBM and the VBM.

00:15:34.767 --> 00:15:37.870
And the CBM and the VBM are
determined by the semiconductor

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:38.370
inside.

00:15:43.575 --> 00:15:45.477
Well, you've also got--

00:15:45.477 --> 00:15:49.081
so this is a photon
emitter right here.

00:15:49.081 --> 00:15:52.985
I've made a light
emitter, right?

00:15:52.985 --> 00:15:54.653
But I've also got
a detector, which

00:15:54.653 --> 00:15:56.121
here's what I said on Monday.

00:15:56.121 --> 00:16:01.694
So now, I take an
LED, and I hold it--

00:16:01.694 --> 00:16:02.728
gesundheit.

00:16:02.728 --> 00:16:04.463
And this is what's
inside of the emitter.

00:16:04.463 --> 00:16:05.397
It's the same thing.

00:16:05.397 --> 00:16:06.398
It's a semiconductor.

00:16:06.398 --> 00:16:09.969
But these two semiconductors
have different band gaps.

00:16:09.969 --> 00:16:12.004
That's why the
light is different.

00:16:12.004 --> 00:16:13.973
They have different band gaps.

00:16:13.973 --> 00:16:16.241
Now I've got one
with some band gap,

00:16:16.241 --> 00:16:21.347
and I hook it up to my
voltmeter, and I just look.

00:16:21.347 --> 00:16:24.350
Is there a current or not?

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.453
If this thing
conducts through it,

00:16:27.453 --> 00:16:29.888
it means that
electrons have been

00:16:29.888 --> 00:16:32.091
promoted from here to here.

00:16:32.091 --> 00:16:35.227
And that's something we're
going to talk about today.

00:16:35.227 --> 00:16:37.796
Electrons cannot
move around easily

00:16:37.796 --> 00:16:39.131
if they're in the valence band.

00:16:39.131 --> 00:16:42.368
But once they're in that
conduction band, they're free.

00:16:42.368 --> 00:16:43.702
How do I get them there?

00:16:43.702 --> 00:16:45.437
Well, you've got power.

00:16:45.437 --> 00:16:46.905
Shine red light on it.

00:16:46.905 --> 00:16:47.773
Does it conduct?

00:16:47.773 --> 00:16:49.975
Check the voltmeter.

00:16:49.975 --> 00:16:52.111
I'm literally holding
two semiconductors

00:16:52.111 --> 00:16:56.615
and doing these two processes
right here in each hand.

00:16:56.615 --> 00:16:58.717
It's a power that I have.

00:16:58.717 --> 00:17:01.587
I have semiconductor
making light, semiconductor

00:17:01.587 --> 00:17:04.156
absorbing light, or maybe not.

00:17:04.156 --> 00:17:06.692
If I shine right on this
and I get no current,

00:17:06.692 --> 00:17:11.563
well, it means that the energy
of the photon from a red light

00:17:11.563 --> 00:17:12.998
source is too low.

00:17:12.998 --> 00:17:16.868
Maybe it's able to excite it
up to here but not up to here,

00:17:16.868 --> 00:17:19.371
given whatever the
gap in here is.

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:20.339
You see that?

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:23.308
So the semiconductor has
this powerful-- maybe

00:17:23.308 --> 00:17:26.712
I take a different semiconductor
and red excites it.

00:17:26.712 --> 00:17:30.783
Or maybe it doesn't, so I go
to a higher energy photon.

00:17:30.783 --> 00:17:33.085
With this goody bag,
you're touching and feeling

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:37.289
semiconductor physics
and chemistry.

00:17:37.289 --> 00:17:42.694
Because band gaps in
materials are rare.

00:17:42.694 --> 00:17:45.964
You see, if you look
at the periodic table

00:17:45.964 --> 00:17:52.137
and you classify it by
metals, metals and nonmetals--

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:54.940
so insulators,
conductors, and then

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:58.310
these weird things in between--

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:01.213
there aren't that many of them.

00:18:01.213 --> 00:18:03.048
There aren't that many of them.

00:18:03.048 --> 00:18:07.252
But as you can see, these
are really, really important

00:18:07.252 --> 00:18:09.688
technological materials.

00:18:09.688 --> 00:18:10.355
Why?

00:18:10.355 --> 00:18:13.225
Because this is visible light.

00:18:13.225 --> 00:18:16.929
This semiconductor range
here is in the range of light

00:18:16.929 --> 00:18:18.697
we care about--

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:20.265
visible, UV.

00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:22.534
So I've got an
electronic material where

00:18:22.534 --> 00:18:28.974
the electrons in it can interact
with currents, small currents

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:32.778
even, and wavelengths of light
that are things we can see.

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:35.681
You can imagine how important
this could be, for example,

00:18:35.681 --> 00:18:37.382
for making LEDs.

00:18:37.382 --> 00:18:42.154
But I need a lot more
flexibility in my material set,

00:18:42.154 --> 00:18:43.655
and that's where
chemistry comes in.

00:18:43.655 --> 00:18:46.325
So this is, one, why
this matters for today.

00:18:46.325 --> 00:18:48.927
So, look, I mentioned
that getting red

00:18:48.927 --> 00:18:51.864
was hard back in the
day for color TV,

00:18:51.864 --> 00:18:54.533
because the red phosphorus
were difficult to make.

00:18:54.533 --> 00:18:56.969
For LEDs, it was blue.

00:18:56.969 --> 00:18:59.304
In fact, there was a lot of
work around gallium nitride.

00:18:59.304 --> 00:19:01.373
Here's gallium nitride
in one structure.

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:02.608
Notice this.

00:19:02.608 --> 00:19:03.876
There's gallium nitride.

00:19:03.876 --> 00:19:05.177
It's not on here.

00:19:05.177 --> 00:19:05.844
Darn it.

00:19:05.844 --> 00:19:09.081
Gallium nitride in other
structures have different gaps.

00:19:09.081 --> 00:19:09.748
Oh, there it is.

00:19:09.748 --> 00:19:11.250
There's one.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.452
But the point was
they wanted blue.

00:19:13.452 --> 00:19:14.853
They wanted blue,
and they needed

00:19:14.853 --> 00:19:18.624
to make a material that was
both cheap to make and lasted,

00:19:18.624 --> 00:19:21.793
so it didn't degrade, and
gave you a blue light.

00:19:21.793 --> 00:19:22.861
And that was really hard.

00:19:22.861 --> 00:19:26.498
And that's what the Nobel
Prize was given for in 2014.

00:19:26.498 --> 00:19:29.668
Because without blue, you
can't make white light.

00:19:29.668 --> 00:19:31.570
So there's all sorts
of technologies

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.940
you simply can't go into
with LEDs until you get blue.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:35.741
How did it happen?

00:19:35.741 --> 00:19:38.810
It happened because of chemistry
because people figured out

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:41.947
how to change the how to take
one element another element

00:19:41.947 --> 00:19:43.916
and then maybe
alloy them together.

00:19:43.916 --> 00:19:46.318
Maybe you take gallium nitride
and you alloy a little bit

00:19:46.318 --> 00:19:49.054
of aluminum in here.

00:19:49.054 --> 00:19:50.756
That means mixing it in.

00:19:50.756 --> 00:19:54.660
Now, all of a sudden, you've
got a different band gap.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:58.630
Band gap engineering is
really the centerpiece

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.800
of the semiconductor revolution.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:04.937
Now, the connectivity.

00:20:04.937 --> 00:20:06.838
We've been talking
about connectivity.

00:20:06.838 --> 00:20:09.474
And we'll talk about
metals on Friday.

00:20:09.474 --> 00:20:10.108
Here we are.

00:20:10.108 --> 00:20:12.211
Insulator's really low.

00:20:12.211 --> 00:20:15.447
Least conducting,
10 to the minus 25.

00:20:15.447 --> 00:20:18.016
Hook your voltmeter
up to Teflon.

00:20:18.016 --> 00:20:19.017
10 to the minus--

00:20:19.017 --> 00:20:21.019
wood.

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:22.788
Low-conducting materials.

00:20:22.788 --> 00:20:24.790
But we're interested
in these, and these

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:25.757
are kind of in-between.

00:20:25.757 --> 00:20:27.759
They're not very
good conductors.

00:20:27.759 --> 00:20:31.263
That's why they're
called semiconductors.

00:20:31.263 --> 00:20:34.933
But the fact of the matter goes
back to what I've been saying.

00:20:34.933 --> 00:20:37.436
And this is the next point
I want to talk about,

00:20:37.436 --> 00:20:43.976
which is I cannot have--

00:20:43.976 --> 00:20:49.748
so in a semiconductor,
there's no electrons.

00:20:49.748 --> 00:20:53.952
If there's no electrons
in the conduction band,

00:20:53.952 --> 00:21:00.525
then there's no
electron conduction.

00:21:00.525 --> 00:21:02.561
Now, I've said this before.

00:21:02.561 --> 00:21:03.462
I said this on Monday.

00:21:03.462 --> 00:21:04.263
I said it already.

00:21:04.263 --> 00:21:05.430
Why not?

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.567
On Monday, I said, well,
these electrons are stuck.

00:21:08.567 --> 00:21:10.068
What does that
mean, they're stuck?

00:21:10.068 --> 00:21:12.571
Stuck in the bonds,
stuck in the anti-bonds.

00:21:12.571 --> 00:21:15.207
They're stuck in states.

00:21:15.207 --> 00:21:20.912
The point is, here, that
this valance band is filled.

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:25.050
Now, if I want an electron
to move through a material,

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.986
which is what conductivity
is, after all--

00:21:27.986 --> 00:21:32.824
by the way, siemens,
conductance, one over ohms.

00:21:32.824 --> 00:21:38.563
If I want electrons to move in
a material, they need freedom.

00:21:38.563 --> 00:21:40.932
They need freedom.

00:21:40.932 --> 00:21:42.134
What does that mean?

00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:45.904
Well, it means that I'm trying
to move through this wire.

00:21:45.904 --> 00:21:47.906
And for an electron,
freedom means

00:21:47.906 --> 00:21:50.175
I can go to any
state that's nearby.

00:21:50.175 --> 00:21:54.379
State, a wave function,
a state of probability

00:21:54.379 --> 00:21:56.648
that I can move to.

00:21:56.648 --> 00:21:58.483
And then to that one.

00:21:58.483 --> 00:21:59.484
And then over here.

00:21:59.484 --> 00:22:00.986
And then there's
is something there.

00:22:00.986 --> 00:22:01.787
I don't want to be.

00:22:01.787 --> 00:22:03.055
I've got to go this way.

00:22:03.055 --> 00:22:04.856
There's a bad thing over there.

00:22:04.856 --> 00:22:06.124
It's scattering me.

00:22:06.124 --> 00:22:07.259
I'll go this way.

00:22:07.259 --> 00:22:09.528
But if electrons
don't have the ability

00:22:09.528 --> 00:22:13.732
to move into a free state,
they're not going to conduct.

00:22:13.732 --> 00:22:17.369
That's why you've got to
get the electron up here.

00:22:17.369 --> 00:22:17.869
Why?

00:22:17.869 --> 00:22:20.138
Because the conduction band--

00:22:20.138 --> 00:22:21.840
I shouldn't really fill this in.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:24.643
I'm just showing you
empty states there.

00:22:24.643 --> 00:22:28.013
The conduction band has
10 to the 24 states.

00:22:28.013 --> 00:22:29.715
Freedom.

00:22:29.715 --> 00:22:31.450
Freedom.

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:33.952
So as soon as an electron
gets up there, it's like, oh,

00:22:33.952 --> 00:22:35.120
I can go anywhere I want.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.054
I can be there.

00:22:36.054 --> 00:22:36.922
I can be there.

00:22:36.922 --> 00:22:40.425
I can move in response to this
field that's pushing at me.

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:44.763
I can conduct.

00:22:44.763 --> 00:22:47.132
I can't do that unless
I have conduction,

00:22:47.132 --> 00:22:49.134
unless I get electrons
into the conduction band.

00:22:49.134 --> 00:22:50.469
This is OK.

00:22:50.469 --> 00:22:54.272
Now, this is why, you can see
right there, metals are clearly

00:22:54.272 --> 00:22:56.808
good conductors, based
on our band picture.

00:22:56.808 --> 00:23:00.379
Because those electrons
near that line there,

00:23:00.379 --> 00:23:02.481
you fill them up, and
they're halfway in a band.

00:23:02.481 --> 00:23:06.418
But right up above there, an
almost infinitesimally small

00:23:06.418 --> 00:23:11.656
energy away, is an empty
state and another empty state.

00:23:11.656 --> 00:23:15.260
These ones you can see,
it's going to be hard.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.462
I'm going to have to shine
really high energy light

00:23:17.462 --> 00:23:21.133
to even get-- or really
high temperatures.

00:23:21.133 --> 00:23:24.336
Again, semiconductors
are special,

00:23:24.336 --> 00:23:27.472
because their gaps are
right in that range where

00:23:27.472 --> 00:23:30.842
I can get electrons to
go from the valence band

00:23:30.842 --> 00:23:33.612
to the conduction band
in different ways.

00:23:33.612 --> 00:23:37.716
And there's two different ways
that I want to talk about.

00:23:37.716 --> 00:23:40.352
The first way is
simply with heat,

00:23:40.352 --> 00:23:43.121
and this is why I
mentioned this on Monday.

00:23:43.121 --> 00:23:45.056
Actually, no matter
what, there's

00:23:45.056 --> 00:23:47.359
some probability for
an electron to have

00:23:47.359 --> 00:23:49.227
a high enough thermal energy.

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:52.130
Yeah, electrons get
hot, too, right?

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:55.133
Everybody feels the heat.

00:23:55.133 --> 00:23:59.571
So that the thermal energy can
be enough to get the electron

00:23:59.571 --> 00:24:01.273
to knock it up above.

00:24:03.975 --> 00:24:09.014
So with thermal
energy, you can get--

00:24:09.014 --> 00:24:10.315
let's see.

00:24:10.315 --> 00:24:14.986
At room temperature for silicon.

00:24:14.986 --> 00:24:18.824
So this is the case of silicon,
which has a 1.1 electronvolt

00:24:18.824 --> 00:24:20.125
gap.

00:24:20.125 --> 00:24:24.463
Silicon, 1.1
electronvolt band gap.

00:24:24.463 --> 00:24:26.598
It's a really important
semiconductor.

00:24:26.598 --> 00:24:30.936
And for silicon, at room
temperature, I got about 10

00:24:30.936 --> 00:24:40.979
to the 10 electrons in the
conduction band per centimeter

00:24:40.979 --> 00:24:42.347
cubed of material.

00:24:42.347 --> 00:24:43.949
This is how we think about it.

00:24:43.949 --> 00:24:48.553
We think about how many of
these do I have per volume.

00:24:48.553 --> 00:24:49.855
It's a good way to measure it.

00:24:49.855 --> 00:24:55.794
These are also an electron
in a conduction band

00:24:55.794 --> 00:24:57.028
also is called a carrier.

00:24:59.865 --> 00:25:04.402
So for silicon at room
temperature, I will have--

00:25:04.402 --> 00:25:08.440
just because I can't help it,
because there's that many--

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:13.512
about 10 to the 10 carriers
in the conduction band.

00:25:13.512 --> 00:25:17.516
Now, carrier is a
carrier of electricity.

00:25:17.516 --> 00:25:19.451
That's why it's called
a carrier, right?

00:25:19.451 --> 00:25:21.486
It carries electricity.

00:25:21.486 --> 00:25:23.154
So those are the ones
that are carriers,

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:24.890
the ones that made it up.

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:26.625
But if I go to 600--

00:25:26.625 --> 00:25:30.328
so now I'm going to
go to 600 kelvin.

00:25:30.328 --> 00:25:32.330
Oh, we only use kelvin.

00:25:32.330 --> 00:25:35.200
Kelvin is the
thermodynamic energy scale,

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.302
temperature scale.

00:25:37.302 --> 00:25:46.411
10 to the 15 carriers
per centimeter cubed.

00:25:46.411 --> 00:25:51.416
This seems like a big number,
but is it a big number?

00:25:51.416 --> 00:25:54.553
What's a big number we know
and like in this class?

00:25:54.553 --> 00:25:55.520
The mole.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:59.658
So like roughly-ish, is
this all a big number

00:25:59.658 --> 00:26:02.227
per centimeter cubed or not?

00:26:02.227 --> 00:26:04.763
It's actually very small.

00:26:04.763 --> 00:26:10.535
But I need them to be there
if I want better connectivity.

00:26:10.535 --> 00:26:13.271
If I want conduction in
these semiconductors,

00:26:13.271 --> 00:26:19.044
I've got to put electrons
up in the conduction band.

00:26:19.044 --> 00:26:22.881
Do you remember, just
as an important point,

00:26:22.881 --> 00:26:26.351
I mentioned the energy of room
temperature, the thermal energy

00:26:26.351 --> 00:26:30.589
of room temperature kBT is
about 0.025 electronvolts.

00:26:30.589 --> 00:26:33.658
That seems way
smaller than 1.1 eV.

00:26:33.658 --> 00:26:34.859
It is.

00:26:34.859 --> 00:26:37.529
But that's because that's
not what temperature really

00:26:37.529 --> 00:26:38.229
looks like.

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:41.299
Temperature is a distribution.

00:26:41.299 --> 00:26:42.267
There's a distribution.

00:26:42.267 --> 00:26:44.402
Now, you don't need to know
that for the band gaps.

00:26:44.402 --> 00:26:46.338
We're going to come
back to distributions,

00:26:46.338 --> 00:26:48.974
Boltzmann distributions, when we
talk about reactions, reaction

00:26:48.974 --> 00:26:51.509
kinetics, later.

00:26:51.509 --> 00:26:55.680
But that's why, at room
temperature, the tails--

00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:58.516
hot means a range of energies.

00:26:58.516 --> 00:27:01.753
Hotter means a wider
range of energies.

00:27:01.753 --> 00:27:05.090
And it's those tails
that have enough energy

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:09.194
to get over the gap and
into the conduction band.

00:27:09.194 --> 00:27:09.928
It's those tails.

00:27:09.928 --> 00:27:12.797
That's why I've got a little
bit of electrons that get

00:27:12.797 --> 00:27:15.166
up there at room temperature.

00:27:15.166 --> 00:27:18.536
Now, if you look at a plot--

00:27:18.536 --> 00:27:20.071
here's a great way.

00:27:20.071 --> 00:27:22.207
There's the log
connectivity of silicon.

00:27:22.207 --> 00:27:23.475
It's from [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:23.475 --> 00:27:26.645
And there's the log connectivity
on this side of tungsten.

00:27:26.645 --> 00:27:28.380
That's a metal.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.583
Totally different behavior.

00:27:31.583 --> 00:27:32.450
How is that possible?

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:35.720
Well, we're going to
explain the metal on Friday.

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:39.024
Today, we're interested in this.

00:27:39.024 --> 00:27:41.159
This one, now we understand.

00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:44.329
Thermal energy excites
carriers into the place where

00:27:44.329 --> 00:27:47.098
they can be free and conduct.

00:27:47.098 --> 00:27:50.335
So as I increase the
temperature hotter and hotter--

00:27:50.335 --> 00:27:52.237
there's the
temperature in kelvin.

00:27:52.237 --> 00:27:54.472
I just gave you
examples from down here,

00:27:54.472 --> 00:27:56.041
and now we're going
higher and higher.

00:27:56.041 --> 00:27:58.510
I can get the connectivity
up orders of magnitude

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.011
higher for silicon.

00:28:00.011 --> 00:28:01.179
You know why now.

00:28:01.179 --> 00:28:02.514
I'm populating.

00:28:02.514 --> 00:28:04.749
I'm getting over
this gap, and I'm

00:28:04.749 --> 00:28:06.718
populating the conduction band.

00:28:06.718 --> 00:28:10.355
So the answer is we just
need to run our semiconductor

00:28:10.355 --> 00:28:15.160
industry and our phones and our
lights at around 1,000 kelvin,

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:15.760
right?

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:17.295
No problem.

00:28:17.295 --> 00:28:18.263
All our devices.

00:28:18.263 --> 00:28:22.600
No, that's obviously
not a good idea.

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:23.702
How do we get around this?

00:28:23.702 --> 00:28:29.541
The answer, such a good
word, it's chemistry.

00:28:29.541 --> 00:28:30.842
Chemistry gets us.

00:28:30.842 --> 00:28:35.013
Chemistry saves us, as always.

00:28:35.013 --> 00:28:40.885
You've got to know
your chemistry.

00:28:40.885 --> 00:28:43.121
And in particular,
this is not the doping

00:28:43.121 --> 00:28:45.356
that you may read about
in the news related

00:28:45.356 --> 00:28:46.257
to Olympic athletes.

00:28:52.197 --> 00:29:02.207
This is the introduction of some
small amount of an impurity.

00:29:02.207 --> 00:29:05.810
An impurity is something
that wasn't there normally.

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:09.981
It's like something I mixed
in that's not normally there.

00:29:09.981 --> 00:29:12.851
If I put a small amount
of something in it,

00:29:12.851 --> 00:29:16.521
then I can tune the properties.

00:29:16.521 --> 00:29:26.131
And in this case, I'm talking
about the connectivity.

00:29:26.131 --> 00:29:29.334
So how does this work?

00:29:29.334 --> 00:29:30.935
If I look at this case--

00:29:30.935 --> 00:29:33.138
I'll do one case here.

00:29:33.138 --> 00:29:37.308
I've got my silicon atoms
arranged in the solid that

00:29:37.308 --> 00:29:38.843
gave me a band gap.

00:29:38.843 --> 00:29:43.081
And again, that is due to
the arrangement of them.

00:29:43.081 --> 00:29:45.049
If I didn't arrange them
in this particular way,

00:29:45.049 --> 00:29:47.085
I might not have that band gap.

00:29:47.085 --> 00:29:50.622
I might not have the
same band structures.

00:29:50.622 --> 00:29:51.856
It depends.

00:29:51.856 --> 00:29:54.993
But this is what I get for
the silicon arrangement

00:29:54.993 --> 00:29:57.929
when the band gap is 1.1
eV, which is the one we

00:29:57.929 --> 00:30:01.966
use in most of our electronics.

00:30:01.966 --> 00:30:04.235
But the thing is, I want--

00:30:04.235 --> 00:30:07.272
so this would be silicon.

00:30:07.272 --> 00:30:11.576
This is just silicon by
itself, valance band,

00:30:11.576 --> 00:30:16.848
and here's the
conduction band, CB, VB.

00:30:16.848 --> 00:30:21.619
Now, I want to add electrons
into this material.

00:30:21.619 --> 00:30:23.655
And so you can just
kind of imagine

00:30:23.655 --> 00:30:25.056
how this would work, right?

00:30:25.056 --> 00:30:29.227
If I just look at a Lewis dot
diagram for silicon-- let's

00:30:29.227 --> 00:30:33.097
just forget about this
structure for a second.

00:30:33.097 --> 00:30:35.433
Silicon has these four
valence electrons.

00:30:35.433 --> 00:30:41.606
So you can imagine silicon
might look something like this.

00:30:41.606 --> 00:30:44.609
And OK, I'm going along,
and I'm going along.

00:30:44.609 --> 00:30:46.411
It's tetrahedral and stuff.

00:30:46.411 --> 00:30:49.914
But now, I'm going
to take one out.

00:30:49.914 --> 00:30:51.382
I'm going to take
this out, and I'm

00:30:51.382 --> 00:30:54.519
going to make it phosphorus.

00:30:54.519 --> 00:30:56.120
What is phosphorus?

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:59.824
It's, wait, where did it go?

00:30:59.824 --> 00:31:01.359
Panic, and then--

00:31:01.359 --> 00:31:03.761
I'm recreating what
happens for you guys--

00:31:03.761 --> 00:31:04.362
I found it.

00:31:07.832 --> 00:31:08.900
That was close.

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:11.002
But phosphorus is right there.

00:31:11.002 --> 00:31:12.804
It's right next to silicon.

00:31:12.804 --> 00:31:15.373
It's group 15 instead of 14.

00:31:15.373 --> 00:31:17.508
But it just has one
extra electron, right?

00:31:17.508 --> 00:31:19.844
So maybe it could
do the same thing,

00:31:19.844 --> 00:31:24.782
but then have this
one extra electron.

00:31:24.782 --> 00:31:27.652
That's doping.

00:31:27.652 --> 00:31:29.420
Where does it go?

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:30.455
It's all about freedom.

00:31:30.455 --> 00:31:32.190
It looks down and
says, wait a second.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.226
There's no states
here I can go to.

00:31:35.226 --> 00:31:36.628
No states.

00:31:36.628 --> 00:31:37.962
I can't go anywhere here.

00:31:37.962 --> 00:31:41.065
So I will have a state, because
I have picked phosphorus

00:31:41.065 --> 00:31:42.200
carefully.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.469
And because of the way the
chemistry and the bonding

00:31:44.469 --> 00:31:46.838
worked with
phosphorus in silicon,

00:31:46.838 --> 00:31:52.277
this will introduce a state
near the conduction band,

00:31:52.277 --> 00:31:53.578
and that's the key.

00:31:53.578 --> 00:31:57.248
So now we've got
the valence band,

00:31:57.248 --> 00:32:00.551
and we've got the
conduction band.

00:32:00.551 --> 00:32:04.622
And what's happened is
there's a new level here

00:32:04.622 --> 00:32:06.291
that is called the donor level.

00:32:11.663 --> 00:32:21.306
That level is where the extra,
extra key electron goes.

00:32:21.306 --> 00:32:22.674
It can't go in here, remember.

00:32:22.674 --> 00:32:24.876
This is all filled.

00:32:24.876 --> 00:32:32.917
So now, I've doped
with P. But look,

00:32:32.917 --> 00:32:37.021
if I've got an electron here,
now this difference in energy

00:32:37.021 --> 00:32:38.923
is really small.

00:32:38.923 --> 00:32:39.791
It's really small.

00:32:39.791 --> 00:32:40.992
I've engineered it this way.

00:32:40.992 --> 00:32:42.093
I've thought about it.

00:32:42.093 --> 00:32:44.929
So if I put phosphorus in
here, that extra electron

00:32:44.929 --> 00:32:48.132
isn't sitting far away anymore
from the conduction band.

00:32:48.132 --> 00:32:52.537
In fact, now it's so close
that room temperature just

00:32:52.537 --> 00:32:53.438
kicks it right on up.

00:32:53.438 --> 00:32:57.475
So effectively, it's
going to go up here,

00:32:57.475 --> 00:33:01.312
because that's a small
delta e between the donor

00:33:01.312 --> 00:33:03.014
level and the conduction band.

00:33:03.014 --> 00:33:05.516
This is how doping works.

00:33:05.516 --> 00:33:07.518
This is how doping works.

00:33:07.518 --> 00:33:08.853
So I put a phosphorus atom in.

00:33:08.853 --> 00:33:10.888
There's an extra electron
that comes out of that.

00:33:10.888 --> 00:33:12.290
The extra electron
needs a state.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.458
It gets a state.

00:33:13.458 --> 00:33:14.759
That's called a donor state.

00:33:14.759 --> 00:33:16.494
Donor makes sense,
right, because it's

00:33:16.494 --> 00:33:20.932
donating an electron
into the conduction band.

00:33:25.403 --> 00:33:28.740
Once I get the electron into the
conduction band, I'm home free.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.609
Because now, I've done what
temperature was doing before,

00:33:31.609 --> 00:33:34.345
but I did it with chemistry.

00:33:34.345 --> 00:33:36.581
And so you could ask
questions like this.

00:33:36.581 --> 00:33:40.585
How much phosphorus do you
need to substitutionally dope?

00:33:40.585 --> 00:33:42.587
Substitution meant
just what you--

00:33:42.587 --> 00:33:44.422
substitution was I
took a silicon atom

00:33:44.422 --> 00:33:45.957
and I substituted it.

00:33:45.957 --> 00:33:48.860
That's substitutional doping.

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:51.129
To substitutionally
dope a mole of silicon

00:33:51.129 --> 00:33:54.899
and get a free electron density
of 5 times 10 to the 17.

00:33:54.899 --> 00:33:56.300
I'm going big here.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:59.804
Because look,
temperature-- gesundheit.

00:33:59.804 --> 00:34:03.975
OK, maybe I'm willing to run my
semiconductor in a really hot

00:34:03.975 --> 00:34:06.744
room, my phone at 600 kelvin.

00:34:06.744 --> 00:34:09.013
I'm still only at 10 to the 15.

00:34:09.013 --> 00:34:11.049
I want 10 to the 17 now.

00:34:11.049 --> 00:34:13.418
How much phosphorus do I need?

00:34:13.418 --> 00:34:14.552
You can solve that problem.

00:34:17.155 --> 00:34:19.757
I'll just write down
a couple of the steps.

00:34:19.757 --> 00:34:24.962
So the volume of
one mole of silicon

00:34:24.962 --> 00:34:32.670
is one mole times
28.1 grams per mole.

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:35.406
Oh, and I got the density
on there, too, right?

00:34:35.406 --> 00:34:41.411
Divided by 2.33 grams
per centimeter cubed.

00:34:41.411 --> 00:34:42.713
How did I get the density?

00:34:42.713 --> 00:34:46.184
I looked it up in
the periodic table.

00:34:46.184 --> 00:34:47.918
And so one mole of
silicon is going

00:34:47.918 --> 00:34:53.157
to give me 12 centimeters
cubed of silicon.

00:34:53.157 --> 00:34:57.094
One mole of silicon is
12 centimeters cubed.

00:34:57.094 --> 00:35:03.000
I need 5 times 10 to the 17
electrons in the conduction

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:06.370
band per centimeter cubed.

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:09.574
You see why per centimeter
cubed is a good measure here.

00:35:09.574 --> 00:35:13.277
So that means I need
12 times that total.

00:35:13.277 --> 00:35:14.512
So I need 12 times that.

00:35:14.512 --> 00:35:20.618
So I need 5 times 10
to the 17 P atoms.

00:35:20.618 --> 00:35:23.054
Why do I need that
many P atoms, times 12?

00:35:27.792 --> 00:35:30.828
Let me just write my units
here to be very careful.

00:35:30.828 --> 00:35:36.667
P atoms per centimeter cubed
times 12 centimeters cubed.

00:35:36.667 --> 00:35:41.506
That's how P atoms I need
in this mole of silicon

00:35:41.506 --> 00:35:43.007
to get what?

00:35:43.007 --> 00:35:44.175
It's one to one.

00:35:44.175 --> 00:35:48.312
Because each P atom
had one extra electron.

00:35:48.312 --> 00:35:51.082
Those other electrons
are in the bonds.

00:35:51.082 --> 00:35:54.252
They're not going into
the conduction band.

00:35:54.252 --> 00:35:56.120
It had one for one.

00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:59.056
One electron went to this donor
state, went into the conduction

00:35:59.056 --> 00:36:00.358
band per phosphorus atom.

00:36:00.358 --> 00:36:02.860
So if I want that
many carriers, if I

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:06.364
want that many free electrons,
I need that many atoms.

00:36:09.367 --> 00:36:12.003
Well, you guys can look up
the phosphorus grams per mole,

00:36:12.003 --> 00:36:19.810
and you can get that this is
0.0003 grams of phosphorus.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.512
That's really not much.

00:36:21.512 --> 00:36:24.048
You see why it's called
an impurity, right?

00:36:24.048 --> 00:36:25.516
You see why it's
called an impurity.

00:36:25.516 --> 00:36:26.350
That's not much.

00:36:26.350 --> 00:36:29.921
I have all this
silicon, and I only

00:36:29.921 --> 00:36:33.758
need 0.0003 grams of
phosphorus, and I've

00:36:33.758 --> 00:36:39.197
bumped up the number of
carriers by orders of magnitude.

00:36:39.197 --> 00:36:41.365
Not only that, I've done
something really important,

00:36:41.365 --> 00:36:42.934
as well.

00:36:42.934 --> 00:36:44.869
And you can see
this, because now,

00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:51.442
if I plot something
like temperature

00:36:51.442 --> 00:36:53.644
versus number of carriers--

00:36:58.049 --> 00:37:02.453
well, you saw the graph for
temperature before, right?

00:37:02.453 --> 00:37:05.623
It was like this.

00:37:05.623 --> 00:37:08.626
That's the graph from
a couple sides ago.

00:37:08.626 --> 00:37:13.197
But now, if I do it with
chemistry, it's flat.

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:20.204
Until the temperature
gets really hot, I could--

00:37:20.204 --> 00:37:22.373
give myself more room
here, because that's

00:37:22.373 --> 00:37:24.075
kind of the point, right?

00:37:24.075 --> 00:37:24.942
Number of carriers.

00:37:27.578 --> 00:37:32.550
I can make this now flat.

00:37:35.286 --> 00:37:42.426
From doping, from temperature.

00:37:42.426 --> 00:37:47.331
And so you can see,
OK, at a certain point,

00:37:47.331 --> 00:37:50.468
the temperature goes up, up, up.

00:37:50.468 --> 00:37:52.103
And at a certain
point, the temperature

00:37:52.103 --> 00:37:55.706
is going to be so hot that
I'm adding electrons, even

00:37:55.706 --> 00:37:58.676
beyond this.

00:37:58.676 --> 00:38:01.445
But you saw the temperatures
there, 1,000 degrees kelvin.

00:38:01.445 --> 00:38:06.017
No, this allows me to have
a predictable, flat number

00:38:06.017 --> 00:38:09.520
of carriers in the conduction
band no matter the temperature.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:13.324
Just think about how
important that is.

00:38:13.324 --> 00:38:16.093
Semiconductors are kind of
discovered in the 1800s.

00:38:16.093 --> 00:38:19.897
But it wasn't until
physicists and chemists

00:38:19.897 --> 00:38:22.733
were able to
control the band gap

00:38:22.733 --> 00:38:27.104
and how to put carriers in
and out of it until they could

00:38:27.104 --> 00:38:29.273
really work on the
semiconductor revolution, which

00:38:29.273 --> 00:38:32.476
is what led to Snapchat.

00:38:32.476 --> 00:38:33.044
Did I get it?

00:38:33.044 --> 00:38:35.913
Snapchat is still used.

00:38:35.913 --> 00:38:36.847
I knew that.

00:38:36.847 --> 00:38:38.983
Slack.

00:38:38.983 --> 00:38:43.621
Now, you can do the same
thing going the other way.

00:38:43.621 --> 00:38:46.957
You can do the same thing
going the other way.

00:38:46.957 --> 00:38:52.963
If I now put gallium, so if
I dope with gallium, well,

00:38:52.963 --> 00:39:02.640
you see, gallium has
one too few electrons.

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:06.610
And so if I dope with
gallium, what I have--

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:08.913
so the gallium is there.

00:39:08.913 --> 00:39:09.814
Where is it?

00:39:09.814 --> 00:39:11.282
It's on the other side.

00:39:11.282 --> 00:39:12.917
Where's silicon?

00:39:12.917 --> 00:39:14.719
Silicon.

00:39:14.719 --> 00:39:17.488
Phosphorus is over
there, group 15.

00:39:17.488 --> 00:39:18.823
Silicon is group 14.

00:39:18.823 --> 00:39:21.292
Gallium is group 13.

00:39:21.292 --> 00:39:22.893
One fewer electrons,
but it really

00:39:22.893 --> 00:39:25.496
wants to make those four bonds.

00:39:25.496 --> 00:39:28.332
The material isn't
happy if you break one.

00:39:28.332 --> 00:39:29.900
And so what does it do it?

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:31.068
It steals an electron.

00:39:31.068 --> 00:39:32.636
It takes it.

00:39:32.636 --> 00:39:36.407
It says, I need an electron to
be happy, so it takes one out.

00:39:36.407 --> 00:39:45.049
And that's called an accepter
level, where what's happened

00:39:45.049 --> 00:39:47.952
is the gallium has
actually pulled

00:39:47.952 --> 00:39:50.054
an electron out of there.

00:39:50.054 --> 00:39:55.126
And that leaves behind
a positive charge.

00:39:55.126 --> 00:39:56.594
This is called a hole.

00:39:59.463 --> 00:40:02.099
That's called a hole.

00:40:02.099 --> 00:40:02.900
Think about it.

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:06.537
If I take an electron off of an
atom, it's positively charged.

00:40:06.537 --> 00:40:08.839
If I take an electron
out of the VBM,

00:40:08.839 --> 00:40:11.442
there's a positive
charge in there.

00:40:11.442 --> 00:40:15.646
That's really important,
because these positive charges

00:40:15.646 --> 00:40:19.283
effectively are like electrons.

00:40:19.283 --> 00:40:21.752
They're like negative
charges, but they're positive.

00:40:21.752 --> 00:40:25.556
So they also can conduct.

00:40:25.556 --> 00:40:28.926
They also can conduct, right?

00:40:28.926 --> 00:40:30.094
And that's really important.

00:40:30.094 --> 00:40:33.197
So these are two
different types.

00:40:33.197 --> 00:40:36.000
And you can imagine the
brilliance of the naming.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:40.204
These are P type, because
it's a positive charge.

00:40:40.204 --> 00:40:44.775
And these are N type, because
it's a negative charge.

00:40:44.775 --> 00:40:46.277
Those are N type semiconductors.

00:40:46.277 --> 00:40:47.111
What does that mean?

00:40:47.111 --> 00:40:49.847
I've used a little chemistry,
sprinkled a little something

00:40:49.847 --> 00:40:50.948
with extra electrons.

00:40:50.948 --> 00:40:53.651
It's got negative charge
in the conduction band.

00:40:53.651 --> 00:40:57.455
P type, P means positive.

00:40:57.455 --> 00:41:00.324
It means that I have
introduced states here,

00:41:00.324 --> 00:41:01.692
because I needed an electron.

00:41:01.692 --> 00:41:02.660
I say, I need an extra one.

00:41:02.660 --> 00:41:04.128
I'm going to grab
it from somebody.

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:05.196
I don't care who.

00:41:05.196 --> 00:41:06.764
And so you create
a positive charge

00:41:06.764 --> 00:41:09.333
in the valence band that
can conduct electricity,

00:41:09.333 --> 00:41:13.404
just like a negative charge
in the conduction band can.

00:41:13.404 --> 00:41:16.607
This is what the entire
semiconductor revolution

00:41:16.607 --> 00:41:17.775
has been based on.

00:41:17.775 --> 00:41:19.610
And really, so much
of the focus was

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:21.846
on simply taking these
elements-- in particular,

00:41:21.846 --> 00:41:23.047
silicon and germanium.

00:41:23.047 --> 00:41:26.250
But then, many, many more, as
I showed you in the LED graph,

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:30.387
many, many more have
been used to make,

00:41:30.387 --> 00:41:33.691
to engineer, these simple--

00:41:33.691 --> 00:41:37.027
what are seemingly simple
properties, the band gap

00:41:37.027 --> 00:41:38.596
and the doping.

00:41:38.596 --> 00:41:40.731
And so I'm not going to
go through this in detail.

00:41:40.731 --> 00:41:42.399
I'm just leaving it
here so you have it.

00:41:42.399 --> 00:41:44.768
But you get charts like this.

00:41:44.768 --> 00:41:49.940
Here, you have, OK,
carbon is up there.

00:41:49.940 --> 00:41:53.143
Well, if you dope it with boron,
you can see that it's P type.

00:41:53.143 --> 00:41:53.711
You see that?

00:41:53.711 --> 00:41:57.147
Because I've got one fewer
electron than carbon.

00:41:57.147 --> 00:41:59.149
So I'm going to grab that
from the valence band,

00:41:59.149 --> 00:42:01.719
create a positive charge P type.

00:42:01.719 --> 00:42:04.421
I could also dope
silicon and germanium.

00:42:04.421 --> 00:42:06.423
I'd say carbon,
silicon, germanium.

00:42:06.423 --> 00:42:07.958
So I'm looking at
this middle column,

00:42:07.958 --> 00:42:09.460
doping it with either side.

00:42:13.564 --> 00:42:16.500
Why can't I put
aluminum in carbon?

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:17.601
Why didn't they list that?

00:42:17.601 --> 00:42:20.538
Well, because if I try to put
an aluminum inside the carbon

00:42:20.538 --> 00:42:22.706
network, the network
gets all messed up.

00:42:22.706 --> 00:42:24.008
Doesn't fit.

00:42:24.008 --> 00:42:25.843
So that's also important.

00:42:25.843 --> 00:42:27.912
Can it fit?

00:42:27.912 --> 00:42:31.882
But it's through this that
the semiconductor revolution

00:42:31.882 --> 00:42:32.683
started.

00:42:32.683 --> 00:42:35.653
It's only through this,
this kind of control

00:42:35.653 --> 00:42:38.122
and this knowledge
of atomic orbitals,

00:42:38.122 --> 00:42:43.093
really, if you think about it,
and the structure of the atom.

00:42:43.093 --> 00:42:44.995
Now, I'm not going to
talk about transistors,

00:42:44.995 --> 00:42:50.134
but this did lead to the
development of the transistor.

00:42:50.134 --> 00:42:51.735
There's a really
nice video there.

00:42:51.735 --> 00:42:53.070
It's, like, eight, nine minutes.

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:56.840
I suggest you wait until
your Friday night social.

00:42:56.840 --> 00:43:01.879
But if I take a P type
and I take an N type

00:43:01.879 --> 00:43:03.914
and I take a P type and
I put them together,

00:43:03.914 --> 00:43:07.718
some really important
things happen.

00:43:07.718 --> 00:43:10.421
So important that it
led to this, which

00:43:10.421 --> 00:43:12.957
is the very first transistor.

00:43:12.957 --> 00:43:14.625
That's a transistor.

00:43:14.625 --> 00:43:18.162
It's a dope semiconductor,
one type, next to a dope

00:43:18.162 --> 00:43:21.231
semiconductor, another type.

00:43:21.231 --> 00:43:23.000
Now, those are
the three people--

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:25.002
Bardeen, Shockley,
and Brattain--

00:43:25.002 --> 00:43:27.605
who won the Nobel
Prize for this work.

00:43:27.605 --> 00:43:30.040
And that's the first transistor.

00:43:30.040 --> 00:43:34.678
Does anybody know how many
transistors we make today?

00:43:34.678 --> 00:43:37.581
Per second.

00:43:37.581 --> 00:43:40.751
That's the only time
metric we can use.

00:43:40.751 --> 00:43:43.721
Yeah, it's something
around 10 trillion.

00:43:43.721 --> 00:43:47.992
We make 10 trillion
transistors per second today.

00:43:47.992 --> 00:43:48.859
This is a big deal.

00:43:48.859 --> 00:43:51.462
This started it all, and
it's all about chemistry

00:43:51.462 --> 00:43:55.165
and the physics of this device.

00:43:55.165 --> 00:43:58.068
This led to diodes
and photodiodes.

00:43:58.068 --> 00:44:01.805
This led to the
whole revolution.

00:44:01.805 --> 00:44:09.313
And what I want to point out
here is that this also led--

00:44:09.313 --> 00:44:11.415
it called on chemistry.

00:44:11.415 --> 00:44:12.449
So I love this chart.

00:44:12.449 --> 00:44:13.984
Unfortunately, they
didn't update it,

00:44:13.984 --> 00:44:18.322
but this is an Intel chart
that they used to show.

00:44:18.322 --> 00:44:22.092
And what they're showing is
how are they making chips.

00:44:22.092 --> 00:44:24.395
How much of the
periodic table do they

00:44:24.395 --> 00:44:26.263
need to make the current chip?

00:44:26.263 --> 00:44:28.899
Well, in the '80s, there's
almost nothing lit up here.

00:44:28.899 --> 00:44:31.335
There's a little bit over
here that you can't see,

00:44:31.335 --> 00:44:32.436
stuff we just talked about.

00:44:32.436 --> 00:44:34.471
But look at the '90s
and look at the 2000s.

00:44:34.471 --> 00:44:38.942
And today, it's 75% of this.

00:44:38.942 --> 00:44:42.046
It's literally 75% of
this is in your phone.

00:44:42.046 --> 00:44:42.546
Yeah.

00:44:42.546 --> 00:44:44.114
Why?

00:44:44.114 --> 00:44:47.251
The reason is exactly what
we've talked about today,

00:44:47.251 --> 00:44:50.120
because they need
more and more and more

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:53.991
flexibility and ways
to tune the band gap

00:44:53.991 --> 00:44:57.127
and the semiconducting
properties of these materials

00:44:57.127 --> 00:44:57.828
and the doping.

00:44:57.828 --> 00:44:58.929
Why do they need new ways?

00:44:58.929 --> 00:45:01.365
Because they're making
them so darn small.

00:45:01.365 --> 00:45:03.600
They're so small, it
gets harder to figure out

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:05.803
how to do it right, how to
do it in a way that's also

00:45:05.803 --> 00:45:09.106
stable over time, for example.

00:45:09.106 --> 00:45:11.108
So they need new chemistry.

00:45:11.108 --> 00:45:14.078
This is a call to action
to the field of chemistry,

00:45:14.078 --> 00:45:17.948
and chemistry responded.

00:45:17.948 --> 00:45:19.983
And there's the
data on the cost.

00:45:19.983 --> 00:45:21.452
I thought that was kind of cool.

00:45:21.452 --> 00:45:26.457
So these are orders of
magnitude of cost and number

00:45:26.457 --> 00:45:28.125
of transistors.

00:45:28.125 --> 00:45:33.664
And I'll just say one
more why this matters.

00:45:33.664 --> 00:45:36.533
Because the semiconductor
is also the same thing

00:45:36.533 --> 00:45:40.471
we use to take
electricity from the sun,

00:45:40.471 --> 00:45:42.439
to generate electricity
from the sun.

00:45:42.439 --> 00:45:48.746
And you can imagine, you are
doing that in your goody bag.

00:45:48.746 --> 00:45:53.217
You're literally using
this semiconductor

00:45:53.217 --> 00:45:58.589
to generate electricity
by shining light on it.

00:45:58.589 --> 00:45:59.490
Why is this important?

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:00.891
Well, I like this comparison.

00:46:00.891 --> 00:46:03.794
All the coal, oil, and
gas known to humans

00:46:03.794 --> 00:46:07.364
is what you get from the
sun in about 20 days.

00:46:07.364 --> 00:46:08.132
This is the sun.

00:46:08.132 --> 00:46:10.567
Now, we've seen the sun before.

00:46:10.567 --> 00:46:13.070
This is how we see
it in our class.

00:46:13.070 --> 00:46:16.874
The sun is a spectrum
of different wavelengths

00:46:16.874 --> 00:46:18.008
and intensities.

00:46:18.008 --> 00:46:20.377
And this is already
on planet Earth,

00:46:20.377 --> 00:46:22.513
because you can see
those chemistries that

00:46:22.513 --> 00:46:25.149
are in the atmosphere
have already absorbed.

00:46:25.149 --> 00:46:28.485
Remember ozone down here,
helping us with UV radiation?

00:46:28.485 --> 00:46:31.455
And out here, you've got a lot
of water and other things, CO2.

00:46:31.455 --> 00:46:32.289
Those are absorbing.

00:46:32.289 --> 00:46:35.592
That's why it
doesn't look smooth.

00:46:35.592 --> 00:46:39.096
But the point is, I want to
semiconductor to absorb as much

00:46:39.096 --> 00:46:40.164
of this as possible.

00:46:40.164 --> 00:46:43.367
If I want a good solar cell,
I should absorb as much

00:46:43.367 --> 00:46:46.036
of this as possible.

00:46:46.036 --> 00:46:48.906
But the problem is that it's
a constrained optimization

00:46:48.906 --> 00:46:50.007
problem.

00:46:50.007 --> 00:46:52.242
So let's see, if I--

00:46:52.242 --> 00:46:53.043
I'll just use this.

00:46:57.548 --> 00:47:00.284
This goes back to
Bohr, by the way.

00:47:00.284 --> 00:47:01.952
Remember, Bohr, I'm
absorbing light.

00:47:01.952 --> 00:47:03.620
It's just now, I've
got an actual solid.

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:06.423
I don't have an atom.

00:47:06.423 --> 00:47:08.392
I've got a solid, which
is what you need if you

00:47:08.392 --> 00:47:09.526
want to generate a current.

00:47:09.526 --> 00:47:12.329
I've got to hook leads up to it.

00:47:12.329 --> 00:47:14.531
But my solid is a semiconductor.

00:47:14.531 --> 00:47:18.802
And you can imagine, say, well,
if my gap were really, really

00:47:18.802 --> 00:47:23.707
tiny, then any amount--

00:47:23.707 --> 00:47:27.945
almost any amount
of this spectrum

00:47:27.945 --> 00:47:31.915
would excite electrons.

00:47:31.915 --> 00:47:34.952
And I might grab most
of that spectrum.

00:47:34.952 --> 00:47:36.787
I might absorb most of it.

00:47:36.787 --> 00:47:39.790
But the problem with that
is that, then, all of these

00:47:39.790 --> 00:47:43.560
will thermalize, like I
said in the beginning.

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:44.428
Loss to heat.

00:47:47.664 --> 00:47:48.432
Loss to heat.

00:47:48.432 --> 00:47:50.500
So if my band gap
is really small,

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:56.406
I absorb a lot of the light,
but I lose most of it as heat.

00:47:56.406 --> 00:48:00.677
If my band gap is really big,
then there's so much of this

00:48:00.677 --> 00:48:02.112
light that I cannot absorb.

00:48:05.849 --> 00:48:07.751
By the way, also, the
voltage that you get out

00:48:07.751 --> 00:48:10.387
of your solar cell is
essentially this band gap.

00:48:10.387 --> 00:48:12.289
So if I get to really,
really small band gaps,

00:48:12.289 --> 00:48:13.724
I have almost no voltage.

00:48:13.724 --> 00:48:15.626
That's bad, too.

00:48:15.626 --> 00:48:17.027
But if my band gap
is too big, you

00:48:17.027 --> 00:48:18.128
think, oh, I'll
get a high voltage.

00:48:18.128 --> 00:48:18.762
No.

00:48:18.762 --> 00:48:21.665
You won't absorb any photons.

00:48:21.665 --> 00:48:23.867
So it's a constraint problem.

00:48:23.867 --> 00:48:24.968
It's a constraint problem.

00:48:24.968 --> 00:48:28.739
You can actually solve
this, and you can plot--

00:48:28.739 --> 00:48:31.308
in fact, here's a
little animation.

00:48:31.308 --> 00:48:33.176
Energy comes in from the sun.

00:48:33.176 --> 00:48:34.278
It excites an electron.

00:48:34.278 --> 00:48:35.779
This is how PowerPoint sees it.

00:48:35.779 --> 00:48:37.848
Leaves behind a hole,
and you get them out.

00:48:37.848 --> 00:48:40.284
That's a solar cell.

00:48:40.284 --> 00:48:44.121
And this is the chart
I want to show you.

00:48:44.121 --> 00:48:47.557
Because you see, if I take
this constraint into account,

00:48:47.557 --> 00:48:49.893
that I don't absorb light
if the gap is too big,

00:48:49.893 --> 00:48:52.062
but I do absorb
light and it all goes

00:48:52.062 --> 00:48:53.864
to heat if the gap
is too small, it

00:48:53.864 --> 00:48:55.933
means there's some sweet spot.

00:48:55.933 --> 00:48:57.634
There's some sweet spot.

00:48:57.634 --> 00:49:00.170
And that's what is plotted here.

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:02.806
This is the band gap
of a semiconductor,

00:49:02.806 --> 00:49:05.342
and this is the maximum
that that solar cell

00:49:05.342 --> 00:49:07.144
efficiency could be.

00:49:07.144 --> 00:49:09.379
That's the maximum that
it could be for that gap.

00:49:09.379 --> 00:49:12.649
So you can see the sweet
spot is right there.

00:49:12.649 --> 00:49:14.551
That's a thermodynamic
derivation

00:49:14.551 --> 00:49:16.720
called Shockley-Queisser
of the maximum efficiency

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:18.689
you could ever get out
of a single material.

00:49:18.689 --> 00:49:20.457
And notice, if you put
the materials here,

00:49:20.457 --> 00:49:23.360
silicon is 90% of the
solar cells you buy today.

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:26.964
And notice that it's not quite
at its maximum potential.

00:49:26.964 --> 00:49:29.666
It's also not quite in the
middle, where you'd want.

00:49:29.666 --> 00:49:31.568
It should have a
slightly higher gap,

00:49:31.568 --> 00:49:34.004
and you'd get to
higher efficiency.

00:49:34.004 --> 00:49:35.405
But these materials
out of gallium

00:49:35.405 --> 00:49:36.907
arsenide, they do have the--

00:49:36.907 --> 00:49:38.709
what is GS?

00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:41.078
That's not an element.

00:49:41.078 --> 00:49:44.748
Gallium arsenide, it
does have a better gap,

00:49:44.748 --> 00:49:47.484
but it's much more expensive.

00:49:47.484 --> 00:49:48.251
And so we go.

00:49:48.251 --> 00:49:50.587
And a lot of solar
cell research is

00:49:50.587 --> 00:49:53.557
on getting as close to
this point as you can.

00:49:53.557 --> 00:49:54.725
See you guys on Friday.

